net/sched: sch_cake: share per-tin thresholds across cake_mq instances

cake_mq gives every TX queue its own cake instance over one shared
config. The shared divisor (rate_bps / num_active_qs, 999-711..713)
splits the aggregate cap correctly, but the per-tin rate thresholds
inside each instance are still configured from that instance's local
rate alone:

	cake_configure_rates(sch, new_rate, true)

so in diffserv8 each instance derives its eight tin thresholds from
new_rate -- its share -- rather than from the aggregate. A tin served
by N instances is therefore shaped against N times its fair share of
the aggregate, and a burst a single instance lets through is only
bounded by that instance's own share.

Add an opt-in per-tin sharing rule. When tin_share = 1, the instance
that wins the sync tick counts how many instances have dequeued from
each tin within tin_hold * sync_time_ns, and publishes that vector on
the shared config; each instance then configures its tins on the
aggregate rate with every tin's rate divided by the number of
instances carrying it, and finally restores its own hard shaper to its
local share. The aggregate cap is unchanged: the hard shaper of each
instance still applies rate_bps / num_active_qs, only the tin
thresholds move.

tin_share defaults to 0: the tins are then configured exactly as
before, and the only added cost is one branch per dequeue. With the
knob on, the cost is paid once per instance per tick (a publish
counter -- shared_last_checked -- doubles as the generation each
instance has already applied), not once per dequeue.

Two bounds are worth stating. tin_cnt is at most CAKE_MAX_TINS (8) and
the vector is CAKE_MAX_TINS wide, so the count cannot overflow the
array. And cake_set_rate with mtu == 0 returns early after writing the
tin rate fields, which is exactly the load-bearing part here; the
per-tin division skips a zero rate, which cake_set_rate defines as
"unlimited", so no tin can be silently unshaped by a division.

Complements 999-714, which counts what this rule would change: nact
gives the divisor, tin_nact_sum gives the per-tin multiplicity.

Not built, not flashed.

